{{uitech}} {{tocright}}
TOC files are addon manifest files, they define the information, contents, metadata, and loading conditions and behaviour of addons.

Example TOC file:
{{#tag:syntaxhighlight|
## Interface: {{API LatestInterface}}
## Title: My AddOn Name
## Notes: Brief description about what My AddOn does to help people find it
## Version: 1.0.0

# This line is a comment

MyFrame.xml
MyCode.lua
lib\MyCode.lua
anotherfolder\MoreCode.xml
|lang="wowtoc"}}

==Rules==
* The file extension suffix for TOC files is <code>.toc</code>.
* The TOC file and folder name must match.
** Example: <code>\Interface\AddOns\AddOnName\AddOnName.toc</code>
* The TOC file can contain the following:
** Directives as <syntaxhighlight lang="wowtoc" inline>## Directive: Value</syntaxhighlight>
** Comments as <syntaxhighlight lang="wowtoc" inline># this is a comment</syntaxhighlight>
** A list of files as <syntaxhighlight lang="wowtoc" inline>myFile.xml</syntaxhighlight> or <syntaxhighlight lang="wowtoc" inline>subfolder\myFile.lua</syntaxhighlight>
*** Only Lua and XML files need to be listed (not media such as images, fonts, and sounds).
*** Files are loaded sequentially from top to bottom.
*** XML files can also load files with <code>{{api|t=x|Script}}</code> or <code>{{api|t=x|Include}}</code> tags.
*** Backslashes (<code>\</code>) are recommended instead of forward slashes (<code>/</code>) for paths to prevent issues with <code>{{api|t=x|Include}}</code> tags.
*** Whitespace before <code>#</code> will be interpreted as the start of a filename, however, the client trims whitespace around metadata values (after the colon).
* The game reads up to the first 1024 characters of each line only.
** Any additional characters are silently ignored.
* Newly created/added files (and even complete addons) are detected when doing a <code>/reload</code> after the game has started.
** If you delete or rename a TOC file already loaded by the game you will however need a full game restart.

==Naming==
Addons can ship multiple TOC files with different file name suffixes in order to load a different TOC for specific game types or their families.<ref name="WoWUIBug#68">{{Ref web|author=Meo[[File:Inv g fishingbobber 05.png|12px]]rawr|url=https://github.com/Stanzilla/WoWUIBugs/issues/68#issuecomment-889431675|title=Multiple Interface Version Support|publisher=WoWUIBugs}}</ref>

The game first searches for TOC files with these suffixes (examples below), and if none are found to match its game type (or its aliases) it will default to <code>AddOnName.toc</code> without a suffix. Family suffixes have a lower priority; for example, having both <code>AddOnName_<span class="apitype">Standard</span>.toc</code> and <code>AddOnName_<span class="apitype">Mainline</span>.toc</code> will result in the former being used for {{API LatestExpansion}}, and the latter for all other Mainline-family game types.

{{note|Comma-delimited [[#Interface|interface versions]] or [[#Inline directives|inline directives]] should be preferred over the use of game type specific TOC files where possible.}}

{{Template:GameTypes|suffix=}}

==Variables==
TOC files support the use of variable expansions for files. They use the syntax <code>[<span class="apialt">Variable</span>]</code>, and can appear anywhere in a line.

;<span class="apialt">Family</span>
:Expands to a game type family (e.g., <span class="apitype">Mainline</span>, or <span class="apitype">Classic</span>) based on the client's game type.
;<span class="apialt">Game</span>
:Expands to a game type (e.g., <span class="apitype">Standard</span>, <span class="apitype">Vanilla</span>, <span class="apitype">Camelot</span>{{efn|1=The <span class="apitype">Camelot</span> value may be changed to <span class="apitype">Forever</span> in the future.}}, <span class="apitype">TBC</span>, <span class="apitype">Wrath</span>, or <span class="apitype">Mists</span>) based on the client's game type.
;<span class="apialt">TextLocale</span>
:Expands to a locale based on the client's configured text locale (e.g., <span class="apitype">enUS</span>, <span class="apitype">deDE</span>, or <span class="apitype">frFR</span>).
;<span class="apialt">RegionalTextLocale</span>
:{{supports|{{LatestPatchInfo|icon=}} {{g|{{API LatestExpansion}}}} [[{{API LatestVersion|test}}]] and {{forever-inline}} {{g|forever}}.}}
:Expands to a region specific locale based on the client's configured text locale and region (e.g., <span class="apitype">enGB</span> and <span class="apitype">ptPT</span> in the EU region vs <span class="apitype">enUS</span> and <span class="apitype">ptBR</span> in the NA region).
{{notelist}}

{{#tag:syntaxhighlight|
## Interface: {{API LatestInterface}}
## Title: My Cool AddOn
## Notes: My addon is working.

# This will load "Mainline\File.lua" or "Classic\File.lua"
# as appropriate for the client.
[Family]\File.lua

# This will load "Standard\File.lua", "Mists\File.lua", "Cata\File.lua", ...
# as appropriate for the client.
[Game]\File.lua

# This will load "Localization\enUS.lua", "Localization\frFR.lua", ...
# as appropriate for the client text locale.
Localization\[TextLocale].lua
|lang="wowtoc"}}

==Inline directives==
TOC files support the inline use of directives which define the conditions under or behaviour with which their corresponding addon directive or file will apply. They use the syntax <code>[<span class="apialt">Directive</span>]</code> or <code>[<span class="apialt">Directive</span> <span class="apitype">value</span>]</code> with case insensitive values. Multiple inline directives can be used per line, and can appear anywhere in a line.
{{note|For reasons of compatibility, inline directives should generally only ever be used at the end of a line.}}

The following directives are supported inline:
;<span class="apialt">AllowLoad</span>
:Restricts the addon directive or file to in-game (<span class="apitype">Game</span>), glue screen (<span class="apitype">Glue</span>), or both (<span class="apitype">Both</span>) environments.
:{{note|Functionally inoperable for addons, as only Blizzard addons are loaded within the glue screen environment.}}
;<span class="apialt">AllowLoadGameType</span>
:Restricts the addon directive or file to one or multiple specific game types, delimited by commas.
:The possible values are the same as used by the [[#AllowLoadGameType|AllowLoadGameType directive]].
:{{warning|1=If at least 1 game type is specified and none of the game types are recognized by the client, the condition will still be satisfied. For this reason, you should additionally use <span class="apialt">ExcludeLoadGameType</span> inline to exclude game types that are not universally recognized yet.}}
;<span class="apialt">ExcludeLoadGameType</span>
:Similar to <code>[<span class="apialt">AllowLoadGameType</span> <span class="apitype">...</span>]</code>, but restricts the addon directive or file to the game type(s) not listed.
;<span class="apialt">AllowLoadTextLocale</span>
:Restricts the addon directive or file to one or more specific client text locales, delimited by commas.
:The possible values are the same as returned by {{api|t=a|GetLocale}}, eg. "enUS" and "frFR".
;<span class="apialt">AllowLoadEnvironment</span>
:Restricts the addon directive or file to the global (<span class="apitype">Global</span>) or secure (<span class="apitype">Secure</span>) environment.
:{{note|Functionally inoperable for addons, as only Blizzard addons are loaded within the secure environment.}}
;<span class="apialt">Bootstrap</span>
:{{supports|{{LatestPatchInfo|icon=}} {{g|{{API LatestExpansion}}}} and {{forever-inline}} {{g|forever}}.}}
:Restricts the addon directive or file to the bootstrap process for <span class="apialt">LoadOnDemand</span> addons.
;<span class="apialt">LoadIntoEnvironment</span>
:Loads the file into the global (<span class="apitype">Global</span>) or secure (<span class="apitype">Secure</span>) environment.
:{{note|Functionally inoperable for addons, as only Blizzard code can be loaded into the secure environment.}}

Example:
{{#tag:syntaxhighlight|
## Interface: {{API LatestInterface}}
## Title: My Cool AddOn
## Title: My Cool AddOn in Classic [AllowLoadGameType classic]

# This will only be loaded on Standard (retail).
StandardOnly.lua [AllowLoadGameType standard]

# This will only be loaded on Camelot (forever).
ForeverOnly.lua [AllowLoadGameType camelot][ExcludeLoadGameType standard, classic]

# This will only be loaded in Vanilla or TBC.
VanillaOrTBC.lua [AllowLoadGameType vanilla, tbc]

# This will only be loaded under English or French client locales.
EnglishOrFrenchOnly.lua [AllowLoadTextLocale enUS, frFR]
|lang="wowtoc"}}

==Addon directives==
Addon directives are lines that define the information, metadata, and loading conditions and behaviour of addons. They use the syntax <syntaxhighlight lang="wowtoc" inline>## Directive: Value</syntaxhighlight>. Generally, if an addon directive is specified multiple times, the last one (with consideration for [[#Inline directives|inline directives]]) will be used. Those that have exceptions to this rule are noted. Where multiple values are supported, they may be delimited by commas.

===<span class="apialt">Interface</span>===
<div style="margin-left:2em">Defines which interface versions the addon supports. An interface version is a single number representation of a client version (e.g., for {{API LatestVersion}}, the interface version is {{API LatestInterface}}).

If an addon doesn't set an interface version that matches the client's, then it's classified as out-of-date.<!-- TODO: Check incompatible range in Forever and document. -->

{{#tag:syntaxhighlight|
# single value for the latest retail version
## Interface: {{API LatestInterface}}

# multiple values, for retail + classic + classic era
## Interface: {{API LatestInterface|_retail_}}, {{API LatestInterface|_classic_}}, {{API LatestInterface|_classic_era_}}
|lang="wowtoc"}}

